Chapter: GopUsbKbd
  cites Works chapter GopXhci
  cites Kernel chapter Pci
  cites Works chapter GopAhci
  cites Works chapter GopHid
  cites Works chapter GopKey
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 USB HID boot keyboard over GopXhci: the transport half of H2b. On a
 machine with no PS/2 controller the firmware's keyboard emulation
 dies at ExitBootServices; the keys keep arriving as 8-byte boot
 reports on an interrupt endpoint of a USB keyboard. This chapter
 fetches those reports and feeds them through GopHid's decoder into
 the same one-byte mailbox the PS/2 interrupt handler fills, so
 gk-take and every consumer above it are untouched.

 We say:

Section: Keyboard State

  uk-ph-idle : Integer = 0
  uk-ph-armed : Integer = 1
  uk-ph-drain : Integer = 2

 Enumerate what the pair can say, because the interesting state is the
 one no bed here can produce (reek, 2026-08-04, at the cost of a board
 trip):

   ok=n                     enumeration failed. Every bed reproduces it
                            by not attaching a keyboard.
   ok=Y ev=0 sc=0           enumerated, and the interrupt pipe has never
                            completed. THE EMULATED KEYBOARD ALWAYS
                            ANSWERS, so no bed in this tree can make this
                            state, and it is what the ASUS TUF shows.
   ok=Y ev>0 sc=0           reports arrive and decode to nothing. This is
                            kbd-drain's ground, and it is the arm
                            kbd-diag-v16 was written for and never flew.
   ok=Y ev>0 sc>0           the whole path works.

 Two more, added 2026-08-04 when the board answered `ev` climbing and
 `rp=0000000000000000` with a key held: completions arrive carrying no
 data, so the fault is the transfer and not hid-step.

 `cc` is the last completion code, which xhci-wait-xfer returns and
 nobody kept. SUCCESS(1) with an empty buffer means the bytes were
 written somewhere that is not uk-report; SHORT PACKET(13) means the
 device completed with fewer bytes than asked; TRANSACTION ERROR(4) or
 STALL(6) name themselves.

  uk-cell-events : Integer = 8
  uk-cell-scans : Integer = 16
  uk-cell-code : Integer = 24
  uk-cell-arms : Integer = 32

  uk-cell-nonzero : Integer = 40

 The counters above live in `uk-state`, which is a per-keyboard heap
 allocation, so nothing outside this chapter can find them: a diagnostic
 holding no UsbKbd cannot read them at all. These cells are the same five
 numbers at a FIXED address, so a passive reader (the diag ladder's
 `mscalign` neighbour, `DiagKbd`) can separate never-armed from
 armed-but-silent from firing-but-empty without touching the device.

 112..119 IS TAKEN OUT OF THE xdiag REGISTRY, and the registry is the one in
 `OperatorsManual.md`, "The xdiag cell registry", which this block is added
 to in the same change. Nothing arbitrates that array and no gate looks, so
 two owners of one cell is green everywhere and surfaces as a wrong number on
 a photograph of a boot screen: the HID note block sat at 80 until 2026-08-13
 and spent that time showing storage state under device-descriptor labels.
 96..111 is that block, four devices deep, so this one starts at 112.

 The mirror writes sit at the four sites that already increment, one poke
 each, so the idle path that "must allocate NOTHING" gains no allocation and
 no branch. A poke is not a record.

  kbd-cell-magic : Integer = 112
  kbd-cell-ok : Integer = 113
  kbd-cell-dci : Integer = 114
  kbd-cell-armed : Integer = 115
  kbd-cell-events : Integer = 116
  kbd-cell-nonzero : Integer = 117
  kbd-cell-scans : Integer = 118
  kbd-cell-code : Integer = 119

  kbd-cells-magic : Integer = #4B424431

  kbd-publish-bound : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-publish-bound (k) =
    let a = xdiag-put kbd-cell-ok (if k.uk-ok then 1 else 0)
    in let b = xdiag-put kbd-cell-dci (k.uk-dci)
    in let c = xdiag-put kbd-cell-armed 0
    in let d = xdiag-put kbd-cell-events 0
    in let e = xdiag-put kbd-cell-nonzero 0
    in let f = xdiag-put kbd-cell-scans 0
    in let g = xdiag-put kbd-cell-code 0
    in xdiag-put kbd-cell-magic kbd-cells-magic

  kbd-report-live : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  kbd-report-live (a) (i) =
    if i >= 8 then False
    else if peek-byte a i /= 0 then True
    else kbd-report-live a (i + 1)

  kbd-events : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-events (k) = if k.uk-ok == False then 0 else peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-events

  kbd-scans : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-scans (k) = if k.uk-ok == False then 0 else peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-scans

  kbd-code : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-code (k) = if k.uk-ok == False then 0 else peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-code

  kbd-arms : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-arms (k) = if k.uk-ok == False then 0 else peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-arms

  kbd-live : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-live (k) = if k.uk-ok == False then 0 else peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-nonzero

  kbd-buffer : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-buffer (k) = k.uk-report

  UsbKbd = record {
    uk-ok : Boolean,
    uk-host : XhciHost,
    uk-dev : UsbDev,
    uk-dci : Integer,
    uk-ring : Integer,
    uk-report : Integer,
    uk-prev : Integer,
    uk-state : Integer,
    uk-raw : Boolean,
    uk-peers : List UsbKbd
  }

  kbd-no-kbd : UsbKbd
  kbd-no-kbd = UsbKbd { uk-ok = False, uk-host = xhci-no-host, uk-dev = xhci-no-dev, uk-dci = 0, uk-ring = 0, uk-report = 0, uk-prev = 0, uk-state = 0, uk-raw = False, uk-peers = [] }

  kbd-peer-cap : Integer = 3

  kbd-peers : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-peers (k) = if k.uk-ok == False then 0 else list-length (k.uk-peers)

  kbd-peer : UsbKbd, Integer -> UsbKbd
  kbd-peer (k) (i) = list-at (k.uk-peers) i

  kbd-adopt : UsbKbd, UsbKbd -> UsbKbd
  kbd-adopt (k) (p) =
    if list-length (k.uk-peers) >= kbd-peer-cap then k
    else let w = list-push (k.uk-peers) p
    in __record-set k "uk-peers" w

Section: Configuration Descriptor Walk

 The boot keyboard interface is class 3 (HID), subclass 1 (boot),
 protocol 1 (keyboard); its interrupt IN endpoint follows it before
 the next interface descriptor. A zero length byte terminates the
 walk rather than looping forever.

  kbd-find-interface : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  kbd-find-interface (cfg) (off) (total) =
    if off >= total then -1
    else let len = peek-byte cfg off
    in if len == 0 then -1
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 1) /= 4 then kbd-find-interface cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 5) /= 3 then kbd-find-interface cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 6) /= 1 then kbd-find-interface cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 7) /= 1 then kbd-find-interface cfg (off + len) total
    else off

  kbd-find-int-in : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  kbd-find-int-in (cfg) (off) (total) =
    if off >= total then -1
    else let len = peek-byte cfg off
    in if len == 0 then -1
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 1) == 4 then -1
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 1) /= 5 then kbd-find-int-in cfg (off + len) total
    else if bit-and (peek-byte cfg (off + 3)) 3 /= 3 then kbd-find-int-in cfg (off + len) total
    else if bit-and (peek-byte cfg (off + 2)) 128 == 0 then kbd-find-int-in cfg (off + len) total
    else off

  kbd-ep-maxpkt : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  kbd-ep-maxpkt (cfg) (off) = bit-or (peek-byte cfg (off + 4)) (bit-shl (peek-byte cfg (off + 5)) 8)

Section: Boot Protocol

 SET_PROTOCOL is a HID class request to the interface: bmRequestType
 33 (host-to-device, class, interface), bRequest 11, wValue 0 for the
 boot protocol, wIndex naming the interface. Without it a keyboard
 may speak its report-descriptor protocol, whose layout this driver
 never parses.

  usb-set-protocol : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  usb-set-protocol (xh) (dev) (iface) (proto) =
    xhci-ctrl-nodata xh dev 33 11 proto iface

 SET_IDLE (HID 1.11 7.2.4) is deliberately NOT sent. HID 1.11 F.3
 requires a boot keyboard to send a report on EVERY interrupt poll BY
 DEFAULT; Set_Idle duration 0 is the one request that overrides that
 guarantee into report-only-on-change silence. A driver that re-arms
 its ring continuously wants the every-poll default -- duplicate
 reports cost one compare in the drain -- and a quirky device may
 over-honor duration 0 as "never report", which is the shape the
 2026-08-03 ASUS flights measured (interrupt polls NAKed for 90 s
 with a key held while the firmware polled the same device fine).

Section: Connect

  kbd-connect : [Device.Port] UsbKbd
  kbd-connect = act
    scan <- xhci-connect-scan
    kbd-hosts scan 0 0
  end

  kbd-hosts : PciScanResult, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] UsbKbd
  kbd-hosts (scan) (from) (ord) = act
    if ord >= xhci-max-hosts then kbd-no-kbd
    else kbd-hosts-at scan (pci-index-class scan xhci-pci-class xhci-pci-subclass from) ord
  end

  kbd-hosts-at : PciScanResult, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] UsbKbd
  kbd-hosts-at (scan) (idx) (ord) = act
    if idx < 0 then kbd-no-kbd
    else kbd-hosts-step scan idx ord
  end

  kbd-hosts-step : PciScanResult, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] UsbKbd
  kbd-hosts-step (scan) (idx) (ord) = act
    xh <- xhci-connect-one (list-at (scan.devices) idx) ord
    if xh.xh-ok == False then kbd-hosts scan (idx + 1) (ord + 1)
    else kbd-hosts-took scan idx ord (kbd-try-ports xh 0)
  end

  kbd-hosts-took : PciScanResult, Integer, Integer, UsbKbd -> [Device.Port] UsbKbd
  kbd-hosts-took (scan) (idx) (ord) (k) = act
    if k.uk-ok then k
    else kbd-hosts scan (idx + 1) (ord + 1)
  end

  kbd-try-ports : XhciHost, Integer -> UsbKbd
  kbd-try-ports (xh) (from) =
    let port = xhci-find-port xh from
    in if port < 0 then kbd-no-kbd
    else let speed = xhci-reset-port xh port
    in if speed == 0 then kbd-try-ports xh (port + 1)
    else let k = kbd-open-port xh port speed
    in if k.uk-ok then k
    else kbd-try-ports xh (port + 1)

  kbd-open-port : XhciHost, Integer, Integer -> UsbKbd
  kbd-open-port (xh) (port) (speed) =
    let dev = xhci-open-device xh port speed
    in if dev.ud-ok == False then kbd-no-kbd
    else let cfg = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let g = usb-get-descriptor xh dev 2 0 cfg 255
    in if g == False then kbd-no-kbd
    else let total = usb-cfg-total cfg 255
    in let iface = kbd-find-interface cfg 0 total
    in if iface < 0 then kbd-no-kbd
    else let ep = kbd-find-int-in cfg (iface + peek-byte cfg iface) total
    in if ep < 0 then kbd-no-kbd
    else kbd-open-endpoint xh dev (peek-byte cfg (iface + 2)) (peek-byte cfg 5) (bit-and (peek-byte cfg (ep + 2)) 15) (kbd-ep-maxpkt cfg ep) (peek-byte cfg (ep + 6))

 The endpoint's service interval comes from its descriptor's
 bInterval and the device speed, through xhci-ep-interval -- dword
 zero of the endpoint context, the field a spec-strict controller
 rejects as a parameter error when left zero.

  kbd-open-endpoint : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> UsbKbd
  kbd-open-endpoint (xh) (dev) (iface) (cfgv) (epnum) (maxpkt) (binterval) =
    kbd-open-iface xh dev iface cfgv epnum maxpkt binterval True True False

 SET_CONFIGURATION resets the data toggle of every endpoint the device
 already has (USB 2.0 9.1.1.5), so a second interface bound on an
 already-configured device must skip it. SET_PROTOCOL is a boot-subclass
 request (HID 1.11 7.2.5) and a non-boot interface is permitted to STALL
 it, so it is sent only to subclass-1 interfaces. A raw interface is
 pumped for its counters and never decoded: its report layout is the
 report descriptor's, which this driver does not parse.

  kbd-open-iface : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean, Boolean, Boolean -> UsbKbd
  kbd-open-iface (xh) (dev) (iface) (cfgv) (epnum) (maxpkt) (binterval) (do-cfg) (do-proto) (raw) =
    let sc = if do-cfg then usb-set-configuration xh dev cfgv else True
    in if sc == False then kbd-no-kbd
    else let bp = if do-proto then usb-set-protocol xh dev iface 0 else True
    in if bp == False then kbd-no-kbd
    else let dci = epnum * 2 + 1
    in let ring = xhci-ring-new
    in let r0 = xhci-set-ep-ring (dev.ud-ctx) dci (xhci-ring-trbs ring) (xh.xh-ctxsz)
    in let ivl = xhci-ep-interval (dev.ud-speed) binterval
    in let ictx = xhci-ictx-single dci 7 maxpkt (xhci-ring-trbs ring) ivl (xh.xh-ctxsz) (dev.ud-ctx)
    in let ce = xhci-configure-endpoint xh dev ictx
    in let nt = xhci-ep-note dev epnum maxpkt binterval ivl (xhci-ep-status ce)
    in if ce.xe-ok == False then kbd-no-kbd
    else if ce.xe-code /= trb-cc-success then kbd-no-kbd
    else let bound = UsbKbd { uk-ok = True, uk-host = xh, uk-dev = dev, uk-dci = dci, uk-ring = ring, uk-report = alloc-zeroed 64 64, uk-prev = alloc-zeroed 64 64, uk-state = alloc-zeroed 64 64, uk-raw = raw, uk-peers = [] }
    in let pub = kbd-publish-bound bound
    in bound

Section: The Pump

 One action per call, from the consumer's own poll loop. The armed
 check is non-blocking: fuel zero means xhci-wait-event looks at the
 event ring exactly once. A device holding the transfer NAKed until a
 key changes state costs the loop nothing.

  kbd-set-phase : UsbKbd, Integer -> Integer
  kbd-set-phase (k) (ph) =
    let w = poke-32 (k.uk-state) 0 ph
    in 0

  kbd-arm : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-arm (k) =
    let w = xhci-ring-push (k.uk-ring) (k.uk-report) 0 8 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-normal 10) trb-flag-ioc)
    in let db = xhci-ring-doorbell (k.uk-host) ((k.uk-dev).ud-slot) (k.uk-dci)
    in let n = poke-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-arms (peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-arms + 1)
    in let p = xdiag-put kbd-cell-armed (peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-arms)
    in kbd-set-phase k uk-ph-armed

 The idle path must allocate NOTHING: this check runs millions of
 times a second from a poll loop on a machine with no garbage
 collector, and a record return is a heap allocation per call --
 polled naively the heap climbs into the stack and __out_of_memory
 halts the machine in seconds, which reads as a dead keyboard.
 xhci-wait-xfer is built for exactly this seat: an integer-returning,
 allocation-free wait that also consults the per-endpoint latch -- on
 a shared host another wait may have already consumed this endpoint's
 completion off the ring and latched it, and a raw ring peek would
 miss it forever.

  kbd-check : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-check (k) =
    let code = xhci-wait-xfer (k.uk-host) ((k.uk-dev).ud-slot) (k.uk-dci) 0
    in if code == 0 then 0
    else let n = poke-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-events (peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-events + 1)
    in let c = poke-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-code code
    in let pe = xdiag-put kbd-cell-events (peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-events)
    in let pc = xdiag-put kbd-cell-code code
    in kbd-set-phase k uk-ph-drain

  kbd-drain : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-drain (k) =
    let live = if kbd-report-live (k.uk-report) 0
      then poke-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-nonzero (peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-nonzero + 1)
      else 0
    in let pn = xdiag-put kbd-cell-nonzero (peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-nonzero)
    in if k.uk-raw then kbd-set-phase k uk-ph-idle
    else let sc = hid-step (k.uk-prev) (k.uk-report)
    in if sc == 0 then kbd-set-phase k uk-ph-idle
    else let w = poke-32 gk-cell 0 sc
    in let n = poke-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-scans (peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-scans + 1)
    in let ps = xdiag-put kbd-cell-scans (peek-32 (k.uk-state) uk-cell-scans)
    in sc

  kbd-pump-one : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-pump-one (k) =
    if k.uk-ok == False then 0
    else if xhci-released 0 /= 0 then 0
    else let ph = peek-32 (k.uk-state) 0
    in if ph == uk-ph-armed then kbd-check k
    else if ph == uk-ph-drain then kbd-drain k
    else kbd-arm k

  kbd-pump-peers : List UsbKbd, Integer -> Integer
  kbd-pump-peers (ks) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ks then 0
    else let p = kbd-pump-one (list-at ks i)
    in kbd-pump-peers ks (i + 1)

  kbd-pump : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-pump (k) =
    if k.uk-ok == False then 0
    else let m = kbd-pump-peers (k.uk-peers) 0
    in kbd-pump-one k

Section: The Fallback

 A keyboard that enumerates and stays silent is the failure this arc
 was built around, and the only honest proof that a keyboard works is
 a keystroke. So the boot offers one: if nothing at all arrives while
 a screen is waiting for a key, the machine hands the controller back
 to its firmware (GopXhci) and the firmware's legacy emulation -- the
 SMM handler that was presenting this very keyboard as a PS/2 one
 until the ownership handoff silenced it -- returns. Scancodes then
 land in the kernel key cell by IRQ, and gk-take reads them through
 the same mailbox the USB pump was filling, so every consumer above
 here is unchanged and none of them need to know which keyboard they
 got.

  kbd-released : Integer -> Integer
  kbd-released (d) = xhci-released 0

  kbd-handback : Integer -> Integer
  kbd-handback (d) = xhci-release-ownership 0

  kbd-take : UsbKbd -> Integer
  kbd-take (k) =
    let p = kbd-pump k
    in gk-take 0
